      "content": "Yes, your starter's microbial community can indeed shift its \"personality\" as dominant species like wild yeasts or lactic acid bacteria grow relative to others based on nutrient availability and temperature. When a specific strain takes over—perhaps because you switched flours or altered feeding schedules—the starter will grow faster, smell more fruity or sour, and react differently to hydration or temperature changes. This isn't sabotage by the microbes, but a natural adaptation of the entire ecosystem to its new conditions, so the solution is often to monitor these changes and stabilize inputs rather than panic.",
